#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187993 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fe2b95-0c68-58f7-a87a-5e4ad3d18e26" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "f8b96980-910c-5e33-b6bf-e1f8532dcdbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10060" :question/problem "Given that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{6}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim N(2,3^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq 6,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(10\\leq Y\\leq 14),$ where  $Y = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_{6}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6026 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6064 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6103 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6141 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6406 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6443 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6480 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6517 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6772 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6808 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6844 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6879 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\nX_i \\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_n\\sim N(n\\mu, \\: n\\sigma^2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{6}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{6} & ∼N(6\\cdot 2,\\,6\\cdot {3}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(6\\cdot 2,\\,6\\cdot 9) \\\\  & =N(12,54).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(10\\leq Y \\leq 14).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10\\le Y\\le 14) & =P(\\frac{10-12}{\\sqrt{54}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{14-12}{\\sqrt{54}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{54}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{54}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.27\\le Z\\le 0.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0.27)-Φ(-0.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0.27)-(1-Φ(0.27)) \\\\  & =2Φ(0.27)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.27) = 0.6064.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(10\\leq Y \\leq 14) = 2\\Phi(0.27) - 1 = 0.2128.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188213 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d240e02-c926-5dcc-9494-315fdd662109" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables In Context"}] :question/id #uuid "c1eefedf-bc2e-5c6c-84e8-0c80aee4a4cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-9267" :question/problem "In a library, each shelf is filled with $20$ volumes of an encyclopedia, plus one bookend. The weight of one volume, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $150\\,\\textrm g$ and a standard deviation of $5\\,\\textrm g,$ while the weight of a bookend, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $50\\,\\textrm g$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\textrm g.$ If one shelf is randomly selected, what is the probability that it supports a weight of more than $3\\,000\\,\\textrm g?$ You may assume that the weights of the volumes and bookend are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 2.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9772 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9778 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9783 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9788 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 2.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9821 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9826 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9830 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9834 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 2.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9861 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9864 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9868 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9871 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X_i$ denote the weight of the $i$th volume, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a bookend. Then, we have\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim N(150, 5^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq 20,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(50, 2^2).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected shelf. Then, since\n\n$$\nW = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_{20} + Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =20\\cdot 150+50 \\\\  & =3\\,050, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =20\\cdot {5}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =504.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW \\sim N(3\\,050, 504).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W > 3\\,000),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>3\\,000) & =P(Z>\\frac{3\\,000-3\\,050}{\\sqrt{504}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-2.23) \\\\  & =P(Z<2.23) \\\\  & =Φ(2.23)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(2.23)\\approx 0.9871.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>3\\,000) & ≈Φ(2.23) \\\\  & =0.9871.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d240e02-c926-5dcc-9494-315fdd662109" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31d6e522-ea81-5a89-8d75-e62a149f4a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1515" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497904} {:answer/id #uuid "4d4f82fe-bfce-5fc7-b26d-b2d5d68ea8fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1056" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497977} {:answer/id #uuid "608251ad-ca2e-599d-b067-b35530881413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498027} {:answer/id #uuid "7932827d-0b13-5355-bce6-923ae6823279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1102" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498089} {:answer/id #uuid "79a59415-e253-515d-b5ff-ac2df63be78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1093" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "608251ad-ca2e-599d-b067-b35530881413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498027} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e990cf7-ee17-56d4-bac7-fe509d55e818" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9f941d6e-0531-597c-97c5-4e948faf202b" :question/math-academy-id "q-110258" :question/problem "In a certain shop, eggs are sold in cartons by the dozen.\nThe weight of an egg, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $65\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $5\\,\\text{g},$ while the weight of an egg carton, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $50\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\text{g}.$\nIf one carton filled with eggs is randomly selected, what is the probability that it weighs more than $850\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ | $\\,0.8729\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th egg, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a carton. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(65,{5}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 12,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(50,{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected carton filled with eggs. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{12}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =12\\cdot 65+50 \\\\  & =830, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =12\\cdot {5}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =304.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(830,304).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W>850),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>850) & =P(Z>\\frac{850-830}{\\sqrt{304}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.15) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.15) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.15)≈0.8749.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>850) & ≈1-Φ(1.15) \\\\  & ≈1-0.8749 \\\\  & =0.1251.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d240e02-c926-5dcc-9494-315fdd662109" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5f4694c-984e-560f-9bf5-4d005a4ac754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9051" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185527} {:answer/id #uuid "23500c3c-24f0-5446-b17f-acf2103a8562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185528} {:answer/id #uuid "32671e29-21ff-5171-bada-a3290f69c8fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9276" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185529} {:answer/id #uuid "506b2362-0b38-5291-bfc0-3ba673c907bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9151" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185530} {:answer/id #uuid "55006ef9-22df-5fdc-8f25-1e174ea1a758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55006ef9-22df-5fdc-8f25-1e174ea1a758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185531} :answer-field/id #uuid "027be15c-3d8e-5694-8ce5-a754e8251921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "76bca3b1-738a-54b2-9eae-c2a2be05e5fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-114465" :question/problem "In a particular shop, eggs are sold in cartons. There are $10$ eggs in each carton. The weight of an egg, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $60\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $5\\,\\text{g},$ while the weight of a carton, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $30\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\text{g}.$ If one carton containing eggs is randomly selected, what is the probability that it weighs less than $653\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9032\\,$ | $\\,0.9049\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ | $\\,0.9082\\,$ | $\\,0.9099\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ | $\\,0.9236\\,$ | $\\,0.9251\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of an $i$th egg, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a carton. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(60,{5}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 10,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(30,{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected carton with eggs. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{10}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =10\\cdot 60+30 \\\\  & =630, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =10\\cdot {5}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =254.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(630,254).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W<653),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<653) & =P(Z<\\frac{653-630}{\\sqrt{254}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.44) \\\\  & =Φ(1.44)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.44)≈0.9251.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<653) & ≈Φ(1.44) \\\\  & ≈0.9251.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fe2b95-0c68-58f7-a87a-5e4ad3d18e26" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0396fc6d-d998-52e8-b095-1ab1f7e19b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(10,25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497807} {:answer/id #uuid "06851348-85f5-57f0-bd1e-28a53a8acad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(25,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497813} {:answer/id #uuid "0939feff-631d-5a84-943c-eeef7faa81ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(25,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497820} {:answer/id #uuid "1165711e-0da1-50e4-80e8-c39d068605b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(\\frac{5}{2},10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497840} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa630cf-f61e-5522-995e-d77a3be16dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(\\frac{5}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0939feff-631d-5a84-943c-eeef7faa81ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(25,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497820} :answer-field/id #uuid "650448ad-2d89-5b9d-bb51-50c7d8573866" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99847d4a-0d57-508f-b2c0-9d057142ac5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143288" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\frac{5}{2},{1}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 10,\n$$\n\nwhat is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{10}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{10} & ∼N(10\\cdot \\frac{5}{2},\\,10\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(10\\cdot \\frac{5}{2},\\,10\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =N(25,10).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fe2b95-0c68-58f7-a87a-5e4ad3d18e26" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c739b6b-35ce-597f-9430-65dd19856da0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4289" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497862} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebfeac7-e54a-54ca-85ee-fe9127f40a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497987} {:answer/id #uuid "6a55dcda-b530-5844-a972-2a28c6beeb2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8577" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498058} {:answer/id #uuid "8645675b-1eaf-5b85-b0af-52db99321ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3577" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498110} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0a9515-6037-5eb4-a1ff-1f653bf5c9f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8645675b-1eaf-5b85-b0af-52db99321ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3577" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498110} :answer-field/id #uuid "cffd4deb-f454-53d3-9291-7403d64f70f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2bacd74e-2117-5657-b43b-3639319ee133" :question/math-academy-id "q-143301" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{14}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(5,{2}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 14,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(70\\le Y\\le 78),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{14}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ | $\\,0.8599\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{14}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{14} & ∼N(14\\cdot 5,\\,14\\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(14\\cdot 5,\\,14\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =N(70,56).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(70\\le Y\\le 78).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(70\\le Y\\le 78) & =P(\\frac{70-70}{\\sqrt{56}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{78-70}{\\sqrt{56}}) \\\\  & =P(0\\le Z\\le \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{56}}) \\\\  & =P(0\\le Z\\le 1.07) \\\\  & =Φ(1.07)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =Φ(1.07)-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.07)=0.8577.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(70\\le Y\\le 78)=Φ(1.07)-0.5=0.8577-0.5=0.3577.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fe2b95-0c68-58f7-a87a-5e4ad3d18e26" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "020bc723-6af1-5316-aaf4-e9de001b448a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7422" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416618} {:answer/id #uuid "0b1b048e-b282-560b-8713-335a9004952e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416619} {:answer/id #uuid "693ff683-c441-5136-b7ec-abf843bb1296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2877" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416627} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd73452-7201-5d58-8fb9-e272ec4a7b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7329" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416634} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c85afb-f62b-5c4e-8deb-61eca859abde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2578" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7c85afb-f62b-5c4e-8deb-61eca859abde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2578" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416636} :answer-field/id #uuid "44ef41a7-faaf-5672-935c-ff24abe14152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c95c5475-5100-54a8-b160-4567ec570e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143339" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(0,{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 15,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(Y\\ge 1),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{15}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{15} & ∼N(15\\cdot 0,\\,15\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(15\\cdot 0,\\,15\\cdot \\frac{4}{25}) \\\\  & =N(0,\\frac{12}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(Y\\ge 1).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1-0}{\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{5}}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{12}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.65) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.65) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.65)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.65)=0.7422.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1)=1-Φ(0.65)=0.2578.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d240e02-c926-5dcc-9494-315fdd662109" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "815d9cd9-9ce6-5961-8d64-1767bb300297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185653} {:answer/id #uuid "9a422df5-ab92-54dd-a1c9-a0ceb47f6704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8822" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185654} {:answer/id #uuid "07bff180-83ad-5844-9ee6-3067e2a819f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185655} {:answer/id #uuid "178a9467-6b72-523c-8a18-5b4ace800e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8030" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185656} {:answer/id #uuid "32275441-e4de-5c6e-8e96-4fa22a6786a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8560" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "178a9467-6b72-523c-8a18-5b4ace800e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8030" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185656} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e3f6c50-ba8d-5d42-8b02-e27814486ca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "531df64f-e13b-5ec0-9ccc-69d48ab88b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-143362" :question/problem "A math test is composed of $15$ exercises. The time that a student takes to solve an exercise, in minutes, is normally distributed with a mean of $6\\,\\text{min}$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\text{min}.$ If one student is randomly selected, what is the probability that they will take between $80\\,\\text{min}$ and $100\\,\\text{min}$ to complete all exercises? You may assume that the times spent solving each exercise are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ | $\\,0.8599\\,$ | $\\,0.8621\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the time spent solving the $i$th exercise. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(60,{2}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 15.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the time it takes a randomly selected student to complete the test. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{15},\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =15\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =90, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =15\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(90,60).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(80<W<100),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(80<W<100) & =P(\\frac{80-90}{\\sqrt{60}}<Z<\\frac{100-90}{\\sqrt{60}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.29<Z<1.29) \\\\  & =Φ(1.29)-Φ(-1.29) \\\\  & =Φ(1.29)-(1-Φ(1.29)) \\\\  & =2Φ(1.29)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.29)≈0.9015.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(80<W<100) & ≈2Φ(1.29)-1 \\\\  & ≈0.8030.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d240e02-c926-5dcc-9494-315fdd662109" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "042295e3-01d7-520c-b096-e70061e6b0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9147" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497809} {:answer/id #uuid "47a985a3-f1dd-5865-bff2-20b4ff772afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0708" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497963} {:answer/id #uuid "5979a90a-2e52-551f-aa16-21610007ae18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0901" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498011} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f98e1b-5dc9-5907-86ef-217b75cf3432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0853" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498273} {:answer/id #uuid "df0a5772-a924-5723-a56b-f2eca0b18e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9099" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5979a90a-2e52-551f-aa16-21610007ae18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0901" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498011} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbc26b25-958d-58de-aac7-dce04e49cb32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77a63100-6b22-5680-ab48-c534778dd5f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-143391" :question/problem "A bouquet consists of $20$ roses. The weight of a rose, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $70\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $5\\,\\text{g}.$ If one bouquet is randomly selected, what is the probability that it will weigh more than $1\\,430\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights of the roses in a bouquet are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9099\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9251\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ | $\\,0.9292\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th rose. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(70,{5}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 20.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected bouquet. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{20},\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =20\\cdot 70 \\\\  & =1\\,400, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =20\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\  & =500.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(1\\,400,500).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W>1\\,430),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>1\\,430) & =P(Z>\\frac{1\\,430-1\\,400}{\\sqrt{500}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.34) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.34) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.34)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.34)≈0.9099.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>1\\,430) & ≈1-Φ(1.34) \\\\  & ≈0.0901.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fe2b95-0c68-58f7-a87a-5e4ad3d18e26" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22a2404a-5457-54f5-9f1e-5112479448f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2266" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497871} {:answer/id #uuid "68bd4d8f-747d-50fd-a2d4-232ab4c0980b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2296" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498050} {:answer/id #uuid "af01c9d1-489a-56fc-8b59-fe3cdccfae2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2358" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498188} {:answer/id #uuid "d27916dc-dfee-536f-be83-7460b487fdfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2033" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498274} {:answer/id #uuid "d9879e3b-c3ea-5e4d-9a07-44aa26061b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2643" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22a2404a-5457-54f5-9f1e-5112479448f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2266" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497871} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a7cc0cb-6474-5b58-baaf-724c624eae6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd6eb665-f20f-5952-b033-241935d01495" :question/math-academy-id "q-152009" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(-0.05,{0.1}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 100,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(Y\\ge -4.25),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{100}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{100} & ∼N(100\\cdot (-0.05),\\,100\\cdot {0.1}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(100\\cdot (-0.05),\\,100\\cdot 0.01) \\\\  & =N(-5,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(Y\\ge -4.25).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge -4.25) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{-4.25-(-5)}{\\sqrt{1}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.75) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.75) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.75)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)=0.7734.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge -4.25)=1-Φ(0.75)=0.2266.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fe2b95-0c68-58f7-a87a-5e4ad3d18e26" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56e78bfe-9b51-5648-bc1f-b326bed0ae1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498005} {:answer/id #uuid "978e1395-42f6-59da-be0b-d636897c86b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4844" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498145} {:answer/id #uuid "b232cea5-5b98-5d1e-aef9-7f111da7c9c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4314" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498197} {:answer/id #uuid "dac1216a-a2d9-5440-8a5e-99b9a41b6363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4972" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498286} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6d116c-6828-5758-80e7-52fc119e9bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4246" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dac1216a-a2d9-5440-8a5e-99b9a41b6363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4972" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498286} :answer-field/id #uuid "effd6099-f4df-5d9b-a019-343ae34a858c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4bd1cff1-6c69-5338-8c54-dfce19778dbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-152011" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(1,{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 18,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(16\\le Y\\le 20),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18} & ∼N(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =N(18,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(16\\le Y\\le 20).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(16\\le Y\\le 20) & =P(\\frac{16-18}{\\sqrt{9}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{20-18}{\\sqrt{9}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{3}\\le Z\\le \\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.67\\le Z\\le 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2P(Z\\ge 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2(1-P(Z\\le 0.67)) \\\\  & =2P(Z\\le 0.67))-1 \\\\  & =2Φ(0.67)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.67)=0.7486.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(16\\le Y\\le 20)=2Φ(0.67)-1=0.4972.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d240e02-c926-5dcc-9494-315fdd662109" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284be8bb-da58-5bfb-9117-3566419ba0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8238" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497882} {:answer/id #uuid "61d21a4f-3736-5e5d-8693-cf92f3ef3f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7704" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498031} {:answer/id #uuid "782f9495-e9fc-5b15-8763-c8baa3500a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7995" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498085} {:answer/id #uuid "956dccfc-7f30-5998-9204-2c6531f74876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7673" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498138} {:answer/id #uuid "9c232066-77f8-5ec7-90b1-5955d51bb494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7839" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "782f9495-e9fc-5b15-8763-c8baa3500a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7995" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498085} :answer-field/id #uuid "df5b89bb-72ac-5d01-aa77-b021461d13e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "227f2a32-4a18-5560-9e21-65c9391f9977" :question/math-academy-id "q-152018" :question/problem "A sports shop sells table tennis balls in boxes of $6.$ The weight of a table tennis ball, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $2.72\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $0.05\\,\\text{g},$ while the weight of a box, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $12\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\text{g}.$ If one box full of balls is randomly selected, what is the probability that it weighs less than $30\\,\\text{g}?$\nYou may assume that the weights are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th ball, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a box. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(2.72,{0.05}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 6,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(12,{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected box of balls. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{6}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =6\\cdot 2.72+12 \\\\  & =28.32 \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =6\\cdot {0.05}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.015.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(28.32,4.015).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W<30),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<30) & =P(Z<\\frac{30-28.32}{\\sqrt{4.015}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.84) \\\\  & =Φ(0.84)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.84)≈0.7995.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<30) & ≈Φ(0.84) \\\\  & ≈0.7995.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
